3. Fix 3 — CONTESTED dark-contrast on Healthcare "+ Add Person" — RESOLVED
   as a probe artifact; PASS, no change needed
============================================================
Reproduced the app's REAL dark-mode mechanism exactly as instructed: on
/whatif, clicked the real `#theme-toggle` button (not a synthetic class
add) and confirmed via DOM read that this set
`html.classList.contains('dark') === true` and
`localStorage.getItem('theme') === 'dark'` — i.e. genuinely exercised
base.js's toggle handler, not a shortcut.
With that real state, clicked [data-toggle-add-healthcare] to reveal the
Add Person form and read the button's OWN computed styles directly from
the live DOM (getComputedStyle), independently of any axe internals:
  dark:  color = rgb(165, 180, 252)   background (walked to nearest
         non-transparent ancestor) = rgb(31, 41, 55)   (--accent = "165 180 252")
  light: color = rgb(79, 70, 229)     background = rgb(255, 255, 255)
         (--accent = "79 70 229")
Computed WCAG relative-luminance contrast myself (independent Python,
not axe, not the worker's math):
  dark:  7.36:1   (indigo-300 rgb(165,180,252) on gray-800 rgb(31,41,55))
  light: 6.29:1   (indigo-600 rgb(79,70,229) on white)
Both clear 4.5:1 with wide margin, matching the worker's claimed 7.36:1/
6.29:1 exactly. styles.css:56 defines the dark `--accent` as
`165 180 252` (indigo-300) with the "axe found 400 fails" design-note
comment at line 50 — consistent with this being a deliberate,
already-shipped token, not new work.
CONCLUSION: attempt-1's reported 2.77:1 (indigo-600 on a dark background)
is a PROBE ARTIFACT. It could only occur if the probe read the button's
color while `--accent` still resolved to the LIGHT value (indigo-600)
against a DARK background — i.e. the `.dark` class was not actually
applied to `<html>` at the moment of measurement (e.g. a `colorScheme:
'dark'` Playwright context option alone, which affects only OS-level
`prefers-color-scheme` media queries and does NOT touch the app's
class-based token switch, so `--accent` stays at its light value while
the surrounding chrome may still look dark from other unrelated rules).
Under the app's actual, real toggle mechanism, no defect exists. No
template/CSS change was required or made for fix 3, and none should be —
the worker's reasoning (changing a non-failing token risks regressing
every other sibling `text-accent` site) is correct and matches the run's
own "don't drop other text-accent uses below 4.5:1" guardrail.

